module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.KeyFormula.Local.OrderComparison
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.EffectiveCartierPrimeDivisor
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.IntegralDivisorMultiplicity
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.WeilDivisorClass.CanonicalSectionOrder
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.WeilDivisorClass.FirstChernClassInjective
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MeromorphicConstant.OrderUnit

/-!
# Generation of the Picard group by an integral effective Cartier divisor

Let `X` be a normal locally Noetherian integral scheme and `E ⊆ X` an effective Cartier divisor
whose closed subscheme is integral, so that `E` is itself a prime divisor `Z_E`. Suppose every
invertible `𝒪_X`-module admits a trivializing section on the open complement `X ∖ E`. Then
`Pic(X)` is generated by the class of `𝒪_X(E)`.

The argument is the one of [Stacks, Tag 0C5G]. An invertible module `ℒ` trivialized on `X ∖ E`
by a section `t` gives a regular meromorphic section of `ℒ` whose order of vanishing along every
prime divisor meeting `X ∖ E` is zero, so the Weil divisor `div_ℒ(t)` is concentrated on `E`.
The only prime divisor of `X` whose generic point lies on `E` is `Z_E`, so `div_ℒ(t) = n [Z_E]`
for an integer `n`, and `[Z_E]` is the Weil divisor class of `𝒪_X(E)` because the stalk of the
ideal sheaf of `E` at the generic point of `Z_E` is the maximal ideal there
([Stacks, Tag 0B3T]). Hence the first Chern class ([Stacks, Tag 0BE7]) sends `[ℒ]` and
`n • [𝒪_X(E)]` to the same element of `Cl(X)`, and it is injective on a normal scheme
([Stacks, Tag 0BE8]).

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor`: the Weil divisor `[Z]` of a prime divisor.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.orderOfVanishingMeromorphic_eq_zero_of_isStalkBasis`: **the order of
  vanishing of a trivializing section vanishes wherever it trivializes.** This is the step the
  source performs in the words "viewing `s` as a regular meromorphic section of `ℒ`".
* `AlgebraicGeometry.eq_zsmul_toWeilDivisor_of_forall_eq_zero`: a Weil divisor concentrated at
  one prime divisor is a multiple of it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.weilDivisorClassOfInvertibleSheaf_eq_toWeilDivisor`: **`c₁(𝒪_X(E)) = [E]`**
  for an integral effective Cartier divisor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.eq_ofEffectiveCartier_of_genericPoint_mem`: an integral effective Cartier
  divisor is the only prime divisor supported on it.

Generation itself is assembled from these in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Divisors/WeilDivisorClass/IntegralDivisorPicardGeneration.lean`,
which is where the trivialization on `X ∖ E` is produced from the vanishing of `Pic(X ∖ E)`.

## Implementation notes

The trivializing section is taken as a hypothesis in the shape `t` together with the assertion
that its germ is a basis of the stalk at every point of the open it lives on, rather than as a
trivialization of the module there: that is the form the order-of-vanishing API of
[Stacks, Tag 02SF] consumes, and it is what
`Stacks.ChernCap42.isStalkBasis_germ_frameSections` produces.

Nothing here is weakened relative to the source: no Noetherian hypothesis beyond local
Noetherianness is used, and the multiplicity-one computation is
`AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheafData.stalk_eq_maximalIdeal_genericPoint` together with
`Ring.ord_of_irreducible` in the discrete valuation ring of [Stacks, Tag 0AG9].

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C5G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C5G)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BE6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BE6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BE7](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BE7)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BE8](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BE8)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B3T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B3T)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AG9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AG9)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02SF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02SF)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory Opposite Order TopologicalSpace

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

/-! ### The order of vanishing where the section trivializes -/

/-- **A trivializing section has order of vanishing zero where it trivializes.** Let `t` be a
section of an invertible module `L` on an open `U` whose germ is a basis of the stalk of `L` at
every point of `U`, and read `t` as a regular meromorphic section of `L` through its germ at the
generic point. Then its order of vanishing along every prime divisor whose generic point lies in
`U` is zero: the constant function `1` is a coordinate of it there.

This is the step the proof of [Stacks, Tag 0C5G] performs in the words "viewing `s` as a regular
meromorphic section of `ℒ`"; `AlgebraicGeometry.orderOfVanishingMeromorphic_ofGenericStalk_germ`
computes the same order for a *global* section and so does not apply. -/
theorem orderOfVanishingMeromorphic_eq_zero_of_isStalkBasis [IsIntegral X]
    [IsLocallyNoetherian X] (L : X.Modules)
    [AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L]
    [L.IsQuasicoherent] {U : X.Opens} (t : Γ(L, U))
    (ht : ∀ (y : X) (hy : y ∈ U), Stacks.Divisors.IsStalkBasis L y (L.presheaf.germ U y hy t))
    (hηU : genericPoint X ∈ U)
    (hs : IsRegularMeromorphicSection X.sheaf L
      (meromorphicSectionOfGenericStalk X L (L.presheaf.germ U (genericPoint X) hηU t)))
    (Z : PrimeDivisor X) (hZ : Z.genericPoint ∈ U) :
    orderOfVanishingMeromorphic X L
        (meromorphicSectionOfGenericStalk X L (L.presheaf.germ U (genericPoint X) hηU t))
        hs Z = 0 := by
  rw [orderOfVanishingMeromorphic_eq_ord_of_isCoordinateAt L _ hs Z _ rfl 1
    ⟨L.presheaf.germ U Z.genericPoint hZ t, ht _ hZ, by
      rw [Stacks.Divisors.toGenericStalk_germ hZ hηU, one_smul]⟩]
  rw [Scheme.ord_eq_iff Z.coheight_genericPoint one_ne_zero, map_one]
  simp

/-- The section of an invertible module determined on an open `U` by a germ which is a basis of
the stalk at the generic point is a regular meromorphic section. -/
theorem isRegularMeromorphicSection_of_isStalkBasis_genericPoint [IsIntegral X]
    (L : X.Modules)
    [AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L]
    {U : X.Opens} (t : Γ(L, U)) (hηU : genericPoint X ∈ U)
    (ht : Stacks.Divisors.IsStalkBasis L (genericPoint X)
      (L.presheaf.germ U (genericPoint X) hηU t)) :
    IsRegularMeromorphicSection X.sheaf L
      (meromorphicSectionOfGenericStalk X L (L.presheaf.germ U (genericPoint X) hηU t)) :=
  isRegularMeromorphicSection_meromorphicSectionOfGenericStalk X L ht.injective

/-! ### The Weil divisor of a single prime divisor -/

open scoped Classical in
/-- **The Weil divisor `[Z]` of a prime divisor**: the coefficient family which is `1` at `Z`
and `0` elsewhere. Its support is a single closed set, hence locally finite. -/
def PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor (Z : PrimeDivisor X) : WeilDivisor X :=
  ⟨fun W => if W = Z then 1 else 0, fun _ =>
    ⟨Set.univ, Filter.univ_mem,
      Set.Finite.subset (Set.finite_singleton Z) (fun W hW => by
        by_cases h : W = Z
        · exact Set.mem_singleton_iff.mpr h
        · simp [h] at hW)⟩⟩

/-- The coefficient of `[Z]` at `Z` is `1`. -/
@[simp]
theorem PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor_val_self (Z : PrimeDivisor X) :
    Z.toWeilDivisor.val Z = 1 := ite_eq_left rfl

/-- The coefficient of `[Z]` at a prime divisor other than `Z` is `0`. -/
theorem PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor_val_of_ne {Z W : PrimeDivisor X} (h : W ≠ Z) :
    Z.toWeilDivisor.val W = 0 := ite_eq_right h

/-- **A Weil divisor concentrated at one prime divisor is a multiple of it.** -/
theorem eq_zsmul_toWeilDivisor_of_forall_eq_zero {Z : PrimeDivisor X} (D : WeilDivisor X)
    (hD : ∀ W : PrimeDivisor X, W ≠ Z → D.val W = 0) :
    D = D.val Z • Z.toWeilDivisor := by
  refine Subtype.ext (funext fun W => ?_)
  by_cases hW : W = Z
  · subst hW
    simp
  · rw [hD W hW]
    show (0 : ℤ) = D.val Z * Z.toWeilDivisor.val W
    rw [PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor_val_of_ne hW, mul_zero]

/-! ### The only prime divisor supported on an integral divisor is that divisor -/

variable [IsIntegral X] [IsLocallyNoetherian X]

omit [IsIntegral X] in
/-- The prime divisor carried by an integral effective Cartier divisor has the underlying closed
subset of that divisor. -/
theorem PrimeDivisor.support_ofEffectiveCartier (E : X.IdealSheafData)
    [IsIntegral E.subscheme] (hE : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor E) :
    ((PrimeDivisor.ofEffectiveCartier E hE).support : Set X) = (E.support : Set X) :=
  rfl

/-- **An integral effective Cartier divisor is the only prime divisor supported on it.** A prime
divisor whose generic point lies on `E` has the same underlying closed subset as `E`, and a prime
divisor is determined by that subset. -/
theorem eq_ofEffectiveCartier_of_genericPoint_mem {E : X.IdealSheafData}
    [IsIntegral E.subscheme] (hE : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor E)
    (Z : PrimeDivisor X) (hZ : Z.genericPoint ∈ ((E.support : Closeds X) : Set X)) :
    Z = PrimeDivisor.ofEffectiveCartier E hE :=
  PrimeDivisor.eq_of_support_eq
    ((supportSet_eq_support_of_genericPoint_mem hE Z hZ).symm.trans
      (PrimeDivisor.support_ofEffectiveCartier E hE).symm)

/-! ### The first Chern class of an integral effective Cartier divisor -/

omit [IsIntegral X] in
/-- **A generator of the ideal sheaf of an integral effective Cartier divisor is a uniformizer at
the generic point of the divisor, so has `Ring.ord` equal to one.** The local ring there is a
discrete valuation ring ([Stacks, Tag 0AG9]) and the ideal sheaf induces its maximal ideal
([Stacks, Tag 0B3T]).

The point is taken as a variable equal to the generic point of the divisor rather than as that
generic point itself: the discrete valuation ring structure is only available at the latter
spelling, and substituting a variable is what makes it applicable at the former. -/
theorem irreducible_and_ringOrd_eq_one_of_stalk_eq_span {E : X.IdealSheafData}
    [IsIntegral E.subscheme] (hE : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor E) (ξ : X)
    (hξ : ξ = E.subschemeι (_root_.genericPoint E.subscheme))
    (hmax : IdealSheafData.stalk E ξ = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk ξ))
    (c : X.presheaf.stalk ξ) (hc : IdealSheafData.stalk E ξ = Ideal.span {c}) :
    Irreducible c ∧ Ring.ord (X.presheaf.stalk ξ) c = (1 : ℕ) := by
  subst hξ
  obtain ⟨hdom, hdvr⟩ := isDiscreteValuationRing_stalk_genericPoint E hE
  have := hdom
  have := hdvr
  have hirr : Irreducible c :=
    (IsDiscreteValuationRing.irreducible_iff_uniformizer c).2 (hmax.symm.trans hc)
  exact ⟨hirr, by simpa using Ring.ord_of_irreducible hirr⟩

/-- The order of vanishing at a prime divisor of a generator of the ideal sheaf of an integral
effective Cartier divisor: it is `1` at the divisor itself and `0` at every other prime
divisor. -/
theorem ord_eq_toWeilDivisor_val_of_stalk_eq_span {E : X.IdealSheafData}
    [IsIntegral E.subscheme] (hE : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor E)
    (Z : PrimeDivisor X) (c : X.presheaf.stalk Z.genericPoint)
    (hc : IdealSheafData.stalk E Z.genericPoint = Ideal.span {c}) :
    (PrimeDivisor.ofEffectiveCartier E hE).toWeilDivisor.val Z =
      X.ord (algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk Z.genericPoint) X.functionField c)
        Z.genericPoint := by
  by_cases hmem : Z.genericPoint ∈ ((E.support : Closeds X) : Set X)
  · -- `Z` is `E` itself, and `c` is a uniformizer of the discrete valuation ring there.
    obtain ⟨hirr, hord⟩ := irreducible_and_ringOrd_eq_one_of_stalk_eq_span hE Z.genericPoint
      (subschemeι_genericPoint_eq_genericPoint hE Z hmem).symm
      (IdealSheafData.stalk_eq_maximalIdeal_genericPoint hE Z hmem) c hc
    rw [Stacks.Chow.ord_algebraMap_eq_ringOrd Z.coheight_genericPoint hirr.ne_zero hord,
      eq_ofEffectiveCartier_of_genericPoint_mem hE Z hmem,
      PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor_val_self]
    norm_num
  · -- `Z` is a different prime divisor, and `c` is a unit there.
    have hgen : (PrimeDivisor.ofEffectiveCartier E hE).genericPoint ∈
        ((E.support : Closeds X) : Set X) := by
      rw [← Scheme.IdealSheafData.range_subschemeι E]
      exact ⟨_root_.genericPoint E.subscheme, rfl⟩
    have hZne : Z ≠ PrimeDivisor.ofEffectiveCartier E hE := fun h => hmem (by rw [h]; exact hgen)
    have htop : Ideal.span {c} = (⊤ : Ideal (X.presheaf.stalk Z.genericPoint)) := by
      rw [← hc]
      exact (IdealSheafData.stalk_eq_top_iff_notMem_support E Z.genericPoint).2 hmem
    have hunit : IsUnit c := Ideal.span_singleton_eq_top.mp htop
    rw [PrimeDivisor.toWeilDivisor_val_of_ne hZne,
      Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.MeromorphicConstant.ord_algebraMap_of_isUnit X
        Z.coheight_genericPoint hunit]

/-- **The Weil divisor class of `𝒪_X(E)` is the class of `E`,** for an effective Cartier divisor
whose closed subscheme is integral: the coefficient of the Weil divisor of `E` is `1` at `E`
itself, because the ideal sheaf of `E` induces the maximal ideal in the local ring at the generic
point of `E` ([Stacks, Tag 0B3T]) and that ring is a discrete valuation ring
([Stacks, Tag 0AG9]), and `0` elsewhere. -/
theorem weilDivisorClassOfInvertibleSheaf_eq_toWeilDivisor (E : X.IdealSheafData)
    [IsIntegral E.subscheme] [hE : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor E] :
    weilDivisorClassOfInvertibleSheaf X (Stacks.Divisors.modulesInvertibleSheaf E) =
      ((PrimeDivisor.ofEffectiveCartier E hE).toWeilDivisor : weilDivisorClassGroup X) :=
  weilDivisorClassOfInvertibleSheaf_eq_of_forall_ord E _
    fun Z c hc => ord_eq_toWeilDivisor_val_of_stalk_eq_span hE Z c hc

end AlgebraicGeometry

end
